# Untitled

Review
Advanced cellulose nanocrystals (CNC) and cellulose nanofibrils (CNF) aerogels: Bottom-up assembly perspective for production of adsorbents
Aref Abbasi Moud
Department of Chemical and Biological Engineering, The University of British Columbia, Vancouver, British Columbia V6T 1Z3, Canada
## A R T I C L E  I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Keywords: Cellulose nanocrystals Cellulose nanofibrils Cellulose Liquid crystals Aerogel Hydrogel Films 3-D printing
The most common and abundant polymer in nature is the linear polysaccharide cellulose, but processing it requires a new approach since cellulose degrades before melting and does not dissolve in ordinary organic solvents. Cellulose aerogels are exceptionally porous \((>\!90\,\%)\) , have a high specific surface area, and have low bulk density \((0.0085~\mathrm{mg/cm}^{3})\) , making them suitable for a variety of sophisticated applications including but not limited to adsorbents. The production of materials with different qualities from the nanocellulose based aerogels is possible thanks to the ease with which other chemicals may be included into the structure of nanocellulose based aerogels; despite processing challenges, cellulose can nevertheless be formed into useful, value-added products using a variety of traditional and cutting-edge techniques. To improve the adsorption of these aerogels, rheology, 3-D printing, surface modification, employment of metal organic frameworks, freezing temperature, and freeze casting techniques were all investigated and included. In addition to exploring venues for creation of aerogels, their integration with CNC liquid crystal formation were also explored and examined to pursue "smart adsorbent aerogels". The objective of this endeavour is to provide a concise and in-depth evaluation of recent findings about the conception and understanding of nanocellulose aerogel employing a variety of technologies and examination of intricacies involved in enhancing adsorption properties of these aerogels.
## 1. Introduction
Cellulose, a linear polysaccharide, is the most common and renewable biopolymer in nature; in fact, cellulose is a structural component of the primary cell wall of plants, algae, and oomycetes [1–3], is an organic molecule with the formula of primary polysaccharide made up of hundreds or even thousands of linked D-glucose units. Cellulose is tasteless, odourless, hydrophilic material with a contact angle of \(20{-}30^{\circ}\) , insoluble in water and most organic solvents; has chirality property [4] (depending on concentration), is biodegradable (can be tuned with embedded enzymes); meltable at \(467\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in pulse tests made by Dauenhauer et al. in 2016 [5]; it can also easily break down chemically into its constructing units while treated with mineral acids at high temperatures [6].
Cellulose materials in the form of lignocellulose (abundant raw material for production of biofuel), bleached pulp (lightening pulp colour), and dissolving pulp (cellulose content \({>}90\ \%\) ) are adequately available in the market; therefore, they are abundant, renewable, and sustainable. With decades of research and exploration, micro−/nanosized cellulose such as microcrystalline cellulose (MCC), cellulose
nanofibrils (CNF), cellulose nanocrystalline (CNC) and bacterial cellulose (BC) with tailor-made properties could be isolated from cellulosic source [7–9]. This nanocellulose material can be further processed, for instance, green and sustainable liberations of CNF by TEMPO (known as 2,2,6,6-tetramethylpiperidine-1-oxy-oxidized cellulose nanofibers (TEMPO-CNF)) oxidation is one notable route [8,10,11]; Carboxylic acid is another significant source of CNC or CNF modifiers [12]; since it can be recycled, the entire method of production can be perceived as sustainable [13,14].
An important subclass of nanocellulose/cellulose products is known as hydrogels that can be morphed into a variety of shapes due to their shear thinning properties. Hydrogels are 3-D networks made up of crosslinked polymers containing a high amount of water in their particle interspace; most hydrogels are not soluble in water [15]; with a range of applications in drug administration, scaffolds in tissue engineering wound treatment, absorbent, thicknesses, and packaging materials, and fabrication of transparent contact lenses [16]. Hydrogel with strong water retention capacity, paired with its porosity, is also useful for simulating the extracellular matrix microenvironment in vivo (a tissue engineering application). CNC in composite hydrogels (mixed with
other ingredients) acts as both reducing capacity of hydrogels such as gelatin to adsorb water but also reduces the evaporation rate of water out of the hydrogel [17]; hydrogel reinforced with CNC could keep half of the moisture even at an elevated temperature close to the boiling point of water [18].
Aerogels are substances generated from hydrogels in which, under benign circumstances, the liquid phase has been swapped out for a gas phase. They exhibit intriguing characteristics and have a very high porosity volume \((>\!90\;\;\%)\) [19]; low density, high surface area and capable of adsorbing liquids. Both CNC and CNF are used to create aerogels, although CNF is better suited than CNC for most foam\aerogel applications due to its higher flexibility, fibrous network, and ability to form a strong 3-D porous network when dehydrated [20,21]. CNC pure aerogels are inherently rigid and brittle, a fact relatable to the inflexibility of CNC rods that are unable to release external stress through deformation and there is no connection between CNCs rods other than the existence of hydrogen bonds; therefore cracks are easily formed [22]. By combining CNC-based aerogels with other non-brittle substances, such as zwitterionic surfactant [23], neutral or anionic polymer [24], amino resins [25], or sol-gel crosslinked latex nanoparticles [26], it is possible to construct them to overcome these restrictions.
Fragility of aerogels can be predicted based on individual components persistence length. Persistence length may be determined for CNC or CNF because the length ratio might influence the transition point at which entanglement could develop in their networks. Persistence length is defined as length divided by persistence length \(\frac{L}{l_{p}}\) where \(l_{p}\) is the persistence length. This quantity may be measured via static light scattering or straightforwardly through directional visualization. \(\frac{L}{l_{p}}{\ll}1\) is therefore for rigid rods and \(\frac{L}{l_{p}}\approx1\) for semi-flexible filaments. Nanotubes and cellulosic fibres are more flexible due to their longer lengths than low aspect ratio particles, which are often formed of inorganic substances like hematite and boehmite. CNC has a persistence length of \(30{-}170\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) [27], which is also influenced by its diameter [28]. The actin biopolymer exhibited a semi-flexible rheology at higher frequencies [29]; at this frequency, the viscoelastic reaction was determined by the stiffness or storage modulus, both of which rely on persistence length [30]. By combining BC or CNF with CNC [31], it is possible to adjust the persistence length as a "ensemble average"; if either of these fillers is combined with CNT, which has a diameter range of \(0.4{-}40\ \mathrm{nm}\) [32], it can effectively encapsulate CNCs and CNF, which has a diameter range of \(5{-}10\;\mathrm{nm}\) , these confinement studies are quite interesting to look into for advanced applications.
Given the wide range of uses for cellulose, shaping it is crucial since some applications are impacted by the form of cellulose-based products; for instance, adsorbent cellulose and cellulose derivative have been used in the form of hydrogels [33,34], films [35,36], beads [37], microfibers [38] and microcrystals [39]; in these applications, cellulose a solid material provide a large surface area as an exchange medium for heat or separation of liquid flowing past them. Using freeze-drying or critical point drying, CNF-based aerogels are practically easy to manufacture shape and have drawn a lot of interest [40]. As aerogels [41–43], these materials find a foothold in energy storage applications, thermal insulation, electronic application including EMI shields and supercapacitors, and sorption purposes; aerogels constructed of chemically cross-linked nanostructured cellulose materials can be used as flexible substrates for a wide range of functional nanoparticles, including hydrophobic nutritional supplements and nanoparticles [44,45]; By chemically crosslinking them, these structures may be squeezed several times for the most effective adsorption-desorption cycles. Porous materials consisting of nanocellulose are usually referred to as "foam" and "aerogel." According to previous research, a (nanocellulose-based) aerogel is "a highly porous solid of ultra-low density and with nanometric pore sizes formed by replacement of liquid in a gel [46]. Foams are sometimes referred to as "solid porous materials with micrometric pore diameters" and their definition is more encompassing within the literature [47].
Literature is rife with publications on shaping and using CNF or CNC with variety of methods; however, a unifying framework such as the current manuscript that can provide a comprehensive outlook on CNF and CNC background, their shaping and connection between process and final properties is currently lacking. This manuscript also uniquely provides a brief statistical analysis based on data in the literature on trends currently established to morph CNF and CNCs into products including aerogels and processing routes. It is noteworthy that CNFs and CNCs themselves are not the focus of the current manuscript, here the main objective is to identify instances where CNC–CNF aerogels (or products) are used to address issues in industry and academics. The nano-cellulose based hydrogel must be shear thinned by shear during the production of the aerogel, the production also involves the generation of liquid crystals (LC), hydrophobization, or surface chemistry tuning of CNCs or CNFs, rate of evaporation, and the impact of ice crystal growth on porosity. These topics are interwoven throughout the current manuscript. These subjects will be investigated along with an emphasis on forming CNCs and CNFs into aerogels. Moreover, throughout manuscript liquid crystalline formation of CNC have been mentioned; review published recently [4] covers most of literature regarding CNC liquid crystalline behaviour and application intended for them; therefore, we refrain from discussing them further here.
## 2. Background
Cellulose is made daily by approximately 3,000,000,000,000 trees and other plants such as bamboo and sugarcane [48,49] and is biodegradable in nature [50]; the majority of cellulose produced in the industry is used for paper making [51]. Cellulose with nano dimension, here called nanocellulose, is preferred due to its superior properties over bulk cellulose [52]. Nanocellulose is a cellulose-based material family that consists of customised cellulose particles having at least one dimension at the nanoscale \((1{-}100\;\mathrm{nm})\) ; thinner than reported values for carbon nanotubes. CNCs, also recognized as nanocrystalline cellulose or cellulose whiskers, and CNFs, also known as nano-fibrillated cellulose (NFC), micro fibrillated cellulose (MFC), or cellulose filaments, are the two primary kinds of nanocellulose [49].
Cellulose processing not in form of nanocellulose is challenging; for instance, to disrupt hydrogen bonds and improve cellulose's ability to dissolve, functionalization processes including xanthation [53], esterification [54], and etherification [55] can be carried out on the hydroxyl group of cellulose to aid in processing. In addition, non-derivatizing solvents may also dissolve cellulose without necessitating chemical modifications, such as ionic liquids [55–57]. These methods do not necessarily require CNC or CNF shear thinning behaviour for processing of cellulose; shear thinning as a subject will be explored further in the manuscript. For either approach, the hydrogel's rheological traits are crucial for their processing; especially, the hydrogel must be viscoelastic (being viscose causes sagging); shape fidelity and shear thinning behaviour is required for easier processability [58–61].
## 2.1. Elementary analysis of aerogels
There are a few metrics applied across literature to characterize aerogels. Researchers determine the density of nanocellulose-based aerogels by weighing and measuring volume; the porosity of the aerogel is then determined as follows:
\[
P o r o s i t y\left[\%\right]=\left(1-\frac{\rho_{a e r o g e l}}{\rho_{c e l l u l o s e}}\right)\times100\%
\]
The liquid adsorption capacity is calculated as follows:
\[
\mathrm{Capacity}=\left(\frac{\mathrm{Wwet-Wdry}}{\mathrm{Wdry}}\right)
\]
Another formula for calculating liquid adsorption capacity is:
\[
\mathrm{Capacity}=\left(\frac{\mathrm{Porosity}\times\mathrm{p}_{\mathrm{liquid}}}{\mathrm{p}_{\mathrm{aerogel}}}\right)
\]
Throughout the text, these equations are employed to provide porosity and liquid adsorption capacity for variously treated aerogels.
