## 4. Rheology
A hydrogel printability and shape fidelity are frequently predicted by its rheological properties [64–66]. Additionally, rheology research can help create the framework for industrial or laboratory-scale procedures. Nonlinear collective properties of colloidal suspensions, which are made up of spherical rod particles suspended in a Newtonian medium, include diffusion, rheology, and sedimentation. Viscosity, for example, can vary by order of magnitude if concentration or diffusion changes [15,49]. Nanocellulose suspension also goes through liquid crystals formation; compared to nanocellulose suspension in isotropic state, the macroscopic flow properties of nanocellulose suspensions are also affected by the formation of LC-ordered domains. Therefore, rheology can differentiate between CNC isotropic state and liquid crystalline state through cues it provides.
Impact of nanocellulose concentration, surface charge, sonication and temperature on viscosity of nanocellulose are other parameters that throughout the years has been studied [67–69] [70]. Phase transition from isotropic to liquid crystalline (chiral nematic) is also possible with examination of flow curves [67,69,71]. To just name results of couple
studies; it was found that increasing concentration causes phase transition from LC to gel [68,72]; through network formation within suspension; moreover, adding NaCl (or other positively charged ions) due to altering repulsion between domains ("tactoids") and CNCs can impact critical concentration at which LC phase formation or gelation happens [73,74].
No matter the production conditions, all types of CNF\CNC suspension exhibit shear thinning characteristics, which means that when the shear rate increases, their viscosity decreases; this observation is regardless of origin of CNCs; charges, length (CNC \(<\) CNF, BC), crystallinity etc. Moreover, at equal concentration of BC and CNF, these suspensions are more viscose than CNC due to their higher aspect ratio and their tendency to mechanically entangle; in case of additive manufacturing (also known as 3-D printing) the less quantity needed for viscosity enhancement might improve product overall sustainability as less processing per gram of nanocellulose hydrogel is needed. Additionally, these aerogels' increased flexibility and inherent physical crosslinking may eliminate the need for substantial chemical crosslinking, further enhancing their environmental friendliness.
Considering biphasic samples addition of NaCl influence flow curves differently, at high shear rates, where all the ordered domains are shattered, the viscosity drops with NaCl addition. The addition of NaCl up to \(5~\mathrm{mM}\) weakens the gel structure and reduces viscosity. Further NaCl addition (10 and \(15\;\mathrm{mM}\;\mathrm{NaCl}\) concentration) causes considerable aggregation and destabilization of the CNC suspensions [75]. Increasing ionic strength up to \(5\,\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{NaCl}\) in biphasic samples decreases the size of chiral nematic domains, resulting in an increase in viscosity at low shear rates. There are still areas that need to be found and explored to better understand the relationship between rheology and microstructure; for
instance, a thorough investigation of nanocellulose chiral nematic structures and their correlation with rheology is required to investigate the influence of changes in twisting angle and pitch length on suspension's rheology [4]. These viscosity modulation practices examined with rheology can provide a roadmap for processing of nanocellulose for aerogels.
Nanocellulose suspensions are also thixotropic, meaning that their viscosity rises to the same level as for the suspension at rest when the shear rate is dropped to zero after a stable shear rate has been applied, achieving an equilibrium level. Thixotropy behaviour is tunable using surfactant [1] and thermoresponsive polymers [76,77] therefore recovery of hydrogel post 3-D printing is faster ensuring better shape fidelity (subject of next section). As a result, viscosity is not only function of shear rate but it is function of time as well, therefore steady-state state should be used for measurements due to significant temporal transitions in viscosity [78]. A Newtonian plateau between the low and high shear rate areas [79] has been recorded in several investigations, and a hysteresis loop has been seen when the shear rate is increased or decreased (or vice versa) [79,80]; hysteresis loop is a test for revealing thixotropic behaviour of nanocellulose. Speaking of the rheological behaviour of CNF (or CNCs); reports [49,78,81] have provided much-needed coverage for both rheological properties of CNC and CNF [49,81]; such as their shear thinning behaviour, large amplitude and small amplitude oscillatory shear and slip behaviour [82]. The most significant rheological models used to describe the rheology of suspensions in the literature are briefly described in Table 3 below.
In the table \(\mu\) is Newtonian viscosity, \(\sigma_{y}\) is yield stress, K is the consistency index, and n is the power-law index, these models are rather simple yet effective in capturing shear flow behaviour of cellulose
particle's suspension; Herschel-Bulkley captures the rheological behaviour of CNC of CNF suspension very well even when they are mixed with other polymers such as polylactide (PLA) or polyvinyl chloride (PVA) [85] [15]. There are reports on the description of CNC rheological behaviour as Bingham plastic fluid [84] as well. Other complicated methods such as Folgar-Tucker equation (not listed in the table) can be found in ref. [15]. A straightforward model that can be used to forecast printability is the power law [61,65]. To test the printability of gel based on alginate, gelatin-alginate blends [85], and poloxamer 407, for instance, this model has been used as an initial screening [61]. Recently, the shear thinning behaviour of nanosilicate/PEG-based gels has also been predicted using this model [86]. The main limitation associated with this model however its limited applicability of range to shear rates between 10 and \(10^{4}~1/\mathrm{s}\) . Moreover, the model does not consider slip effect and assume its linear and steady at medium shear rates. In contrary, because it considers both the yield stress and shear response as well as wall slippage within the needle, the Herschel–Bulkley model is quite helpful in comparison to power law [87–89].
Cellulose with surfactant also displays shear thinning behaviour [1] and peculiar thixotropic behaviour, as an alternative, the addition of non-ionic polymers or surfactants can also cause the sol-gel transition since they increase the yield stresses by increasing the effective volume fraction and bridging of nanocrystals [90,91]. Due to changes in zeta potential and hydrophobic forces, the addition of cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) and sodium dodecyl sulphate (SDS) caused the CNC solution to gel in one report [92]. It is reported that chemical reaction with surfactant did not change length of CNC however the strands became after \(4\;\mathrm{h}\) of reaction made them by \(1\ \mathrm{nm}\) thicker [94]. Similarly reporting hydrodynamic diameter of CNCs reveals enlargement of particles with addition of hydroxyethyl cellulose (HEC), hydroxypropyl guar (HPG), or locust bean gum [90]; similar evolution in hydrodynamic diameter was reported in ref [95]. These changes in aspect ratio of particle can shift gelation and liquid crystalline transition points [4].
Surface modification can alter processing behaviour of nanocellulose gels similar to action of surfactants (a method to adjust chemistry of surfaces of CNCs). Reports on surface modification of CNCs with azetidinium salts considerably increased the viscosity and viscoelastic character of the gels in their restricted capacity [96]. In the same way, unmodified CNCs in dimethylsulfoxide (DMSO) transformed into a thermosensitive gel; however, modified CNCs (grafted with an organic acid chloride) displayed weak gel behaviour, and the temperature had little impact on their viscoelastic behaviour [97]. In one study [98], the level of hydrophobization was tuned through changing alkyl chain length coupled with primary alkyl amines, authors reported sol-gel transition to occur at a lower concentration from modified CNCs, and gels were stronger. These findings suggest that aerogels with hydrophobic properties may respond better than unmodified CNC.
Rheological studies can be extended to cases where CNC is mixed with other components such as PEG [100,101], glycerol [102], gold nanorods or here gum Arabic. Indeed, these studies are needed not only to address whether addition of these ingredients changes microstructure of CNC suspension and therefore rheology but also, they have practical importance as CNC in industry is mixed with other ingredients. Buffa et al. [103] conducted a comprehensive rheological study of the
behaviour of CNCs in water, focusing on the main characteristics of rodlike suspensions that align under flow and can exhibit LC behaviour at rest, in at least a range of concentrations. The effect of adding a dispersing agent (gum arabic, GA) to the suspension was studied, and different surface treatments on the CNC were performed using TEMPO (TEMPO-CNC), chloro(dodecyl)dimethyl silane (DCNC), and trimethylsilyl propyl methacrylate (MCNC), abbreviated as TEMPO-CNC, DCNC, and MCNC. The CNC, TCNC, and GA-added suspensions formed stable suspensions with a concentration range in which isotropic and anisotropic (self-organized) phases existed in equilibrium. The research on gum Arabic is especially important since it may make structurally coloured pigments for food decoration when an edible component [104] is combined with CNC.
Other rheometric measurements other than shear flow, can be utilised to extrapolate more structural details about rod suspensions in glassy or gel states. Notable note should be made of the finding of a negative initial normal stress difference (N1) in biphasic suspension (includes LC phase). When elongated nanoparticles or filaments are oriented with the flow [105,106], they travel in a way that causes N1 to encounter a minimum that can be negative [107]; this is a phenomenon that is also observed in polymer melts and solutions [108]. A derived cellulose polymer in aqueous solution has also been shown to exhibit this behaviour (hydroxypropyl cellulose) [109] [110]. It is known that the positive N1 in the polymer solution or melt causes it to inflate as it escapes the die [111], which leads to uneven extruded shapes. For the development of polymer products without die swell, the negative N1 in biphasic suspension of CNC identical to Carbon nanotubes (CNT) [112] can be quite significant [113,114]. To improve CNC-based materials, more research is required to determine the cause of negative N1.
Other anomalies about the rheological characteristics of the CNC-CNF suspension include the non-application of the "Cox-Merz" rule when salt is added and the presence of liquid crystalline domains; in addition to emergence of loss modulus overshoot in strain amplitude tests for both coagulated and biphasic samples [115]. These rheological anomalies are crucial for processing these materials, as using CNC hydrogel dynamically at the yielding position can be energy-dissipating in the presence of a biphasic suspension. For instance, the energy dissipation of the shell of a microcapsule built of CNC biphasic phases may differ from that of a shell made of CNCs in their isotropic state. Cellulose in aerogel form can produce actuators or origami constructions; these actuator have been used in synthetic tissue, medicinal devices, examinations, and bioelectronics [116] to just name a few. Ionic contacts between CNF and poly ethylene glycol (PEG) can cause a reversible sol-gel transformation when subjected to strain or temperatures increasing [117]; i.e. the bond can break and re-establish after stress is removed. The membranes of the hydrogel can in certain ways act as a layer that regulates ion exchange. Typical CNF-based hydrogels include 0.05 to 6 % CNFs by weight and have a storage modulus of almost 10–100 kPa [118]. Biphasic LC phases does not obey Cox-Merz rule to their presence of a time-dependent microstructure [68,103]; furthermore, these structures are broken when shear is applied, but they are left intact in the linear oscillatory shear regime, hence a divergence behaviour is anticipated. In case of other irregularities, testing for LC using small-amplitude sinusoidal oscillations cannot produce concentration-master curves. For these biphasic CNC suspensions [68], the method of creating a master curve by frequency shifting the curves of dynamic properties acquired at a given concentration, such as storage and loss moduli, does not yield clean curves.
Understanding the microstructure during and after processing requires examining the rheology of CNC and CNF prior to fabrication in either microfluidic or 3-D printing technology. Mechanical characteristics of the product, porosity, shape fidelity, liquid crystalline state, pitch of LC that can provide the structure with structural colour, etc. are all factors that can be influenced by processing and rheology.
## 5. Processing methods
5. Processing methodsThe final form, production procedure, intended application, and mechanical properties of CNC products are briefly summarised in Table 4. Careful testing to ensure that the created aerogel is capable of meeting market standards shows that mechanical properties is essential for all purposes, irrespective of the manufacturing stage. Likewise, the composite hydrogels acquired mechanical modulus in terms of shear, tensile, and compression is comparable to that of human medial meniscus \((1.0\,\mathrm{\:MPa})\), native femoral artery \((9.0\,\mathrm{\:MPa})\), and articular cartilage \((0.4{-}1.6\,\mathrm{\:MPa})\), including both. This demonstrates their capacity for different biomaterials in hydrogel form [119].
## 5.1. Conventional processing methods
5.1. Conventional processing methodsThe final stage is drying the wet gel, which only leaves the cellulose network left once the solvent has been removed. There are three drying methods that are often used: Evaporative drying, freeze drying, and supercritical drying. Xerogels, cryogels, and aerogels are the common names for the materials created by each drying method, with aerogels having the largest pore structure. Evaporative drying with water suffers pore collapse due to capillary forces indeed, Strong surface tension influences can result in the partial collapse of the structure when the solvent inside the pores is ready to evaporate. In these cases, a solvent exchange is recommended before the evaporation stage to decrease the effect of capillary pressures on the collapse of pores. Additionally, cellulose can be surface modified with hydrophobic groups to prevent shrinking. Due to the spring back effect [126], the capillary forces' influence is reduced when hydrophobic groups are present on the aerogel due to the repulsion between these groups and their reduced interaction with the existing polar solvent. Using these schemes, it is feasible to accurately regulate the texture of pores [127,128] if the drying-related processing conditions, such as vacuum, solvent type, temperature, etc., are chosen with care. Noteworthy is that a partial collapse of the network is not essential if pore diameters are in the micro (below \(2\;\mathrm{nm}\)) and meso (between 2 and \(50\;\mathrm{nm}\)) range.Toivonen et al. [129] devised a method for producing aerogel membranes without the need of freeze-drying or critical point drying, using CNF vacuum filtering, solvent transfer, and ambient curing, known as solvent casting (also known as oven drying or normal drying). Increasing the CNF concentration inside the aerogels resulted in a collapsed sheet-like structure with a higher bulk density and lower surface area than previously reported aerogels, which was not unexpected and unaffected by the preparation procedure [130,131]. t-Butanol is among the most popular solvent used mostly for exchange with water because it suffers less change in density of aerogel than water and drying time is less due to higher vapour pressure [132]. The use of t-butanol in solvent exchange can effectively protect better the gel structure than water because it has low interfacial tension, one hydroxyl group that can form a hydrogen bond with cellulose, and many butyl groups that act as steric hindrance to prevent cellulose from reaggregating during. Supercritical fluid drying is the most popular drying technique; thus, the best way to protect the porous network is to carry out the solvent removal phase in this technique at high temperatures and pressures. Typically, supercritical drying is done in an autoclave. The pressure and temperature are raised until they reach the solvent's corresponding critical levels. There are no liquid-vapour interfaces in the supercritical state, hence surface tension and capillary forces cannot disintegrate the structure. Carbon dioxide is recommended to be in place of the organic solvent to prevent excessive temperatures. Due to its low surface tension and lower critical temperature of \(31\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) has a softer processing temperature than an organic solvent [130,134]; however there are disadvantageous associated with this technique such as cost and maintenance of equipment due to operation under high pressure and carbon dioxide feed when compared against other conventional practices.Alternatively, the liquid in hydrogel can be frozen and later using sublimation solvent can be removed from the system leaving porous network behind; the methodology mechanism is based on knowing most solutes and/or suspended particles in water are separated from the developing ice front into the interstitial zone generated by crystals because most compounds in ice have low accessibility; aerogels created have porosities resembling ice crystals [135]. Using just the lyophilized also known as "freeze casting" method, a colloid dispersion or solution is frozen solely in one side while adhering to a pre-set heat gradient [136]. Through the use of this technology, hydrogels with controlled pore design may be dried while a large portion of the mesopores is effectively removed [137].Prior to sublimation, the liquid can be frozen either randomly (called freeze drying) or intentionally (called "freeze-casting"). The hydrogel is solidified with the intention of having the pores develop in a certain direction (1-d, 2-D or radial direction), unlike freeze drying, when positional crystal formation might be arbitrary. Particles are tightly bound together by strong hydrogen bonds and van der Waals forces once ice crystals have formed [138]. This is comparable to the method for producing strong fibres that create uniform and fine threads from a little amount of CNF in water by combining hydrodynamic alignment with a sol-gel transition, but it takes a less complex approach [139]. In this case, however, the apparatus is simpler.Table 5 lists the cross-linking techniques (physical and chemical) for aerogels (categorised into chemical and physical cross-linking), method of aerogel production, the greatest porosity attained, the density and an application for the created aerogel. Most of the aerogels listed had high porosities, ranging from 75 to \(99\,\%\) across the table. This suggests that a variety of processing methods may be employed to produce extremely light aerogels. It is fascinating because some applications require specific porosity, pore shape, and pore connectivity for the best attributes; this necessitates a detailed examination of the microscope study of the aerogel developed by the researchers to offer a clearer picture. For example, it is preferable for pores in thermal insulation to be close together and not interconnected in order to reduce thermal conductivity [140]. On the other hand, interconnectivity of pores is necessary for adsorption and tissue engineering because it promotes chemical signalling between cells and their migration. According to statistical evidence, Table 5 also demonstrates that most applications for nanocellulose aerogels are for adsorbent and thermal insulation
