Supercritical fluid drying is the most popular drying technique; thus,
the best way to protect the porous network is to carry out the solvent removal phase in this technique at high temperatures and pressures. Typically, supercritical drying is done in an autoclave. The pressure and temperature are raised until they reach the solvent's corresponding critical levels. There are no liquid-vapour interfaces in the supercritical state, hence surface tension and capillary forces cannot disintegrate the structure. Carbon dioxide is recommended to be in place of the organic solvent to prevent excessive temperatures. Due to its low surface tension and lower critical temperature of \(31\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) has a softer processing temperature than an organic solvent [130,134]; however there are disadvantageous associated with this technique such as cost and maintenance of equipment due to operation under high pressure and carbon dioxide feed when compared against other conventional practices.
Alternatively, the liquid in hydrogel can be frozen and later using sublimation solvent can be removed from the system leaving porous network behind; the methodology mechanism is based on knowing most solutes and/or suspended particles in water are separated from the developing ice front into the interstitial zone generated by crystals because most compounds in ice have low accessibility; aerogels created have porosities resembling ice crystals [135]. Using just the lyophilized also known as "freeze casting" method, a colloid dispersion or solution is frozen solely in one side while adhering to a pre-set heat gradient [136]. Through the use of this technology, hydrogels with controlled pore design may be dried while a large portion of the mesopores is effectively removed [137].
Prior to sublimation, the liquid can be frozen either randomly (called freeze drying) or intentionally (called "freeze-casting"). The hydrogel is solidified with the intention of having the pores develop in a certain direction (1-d, 2-D or radial direction), unlike freeze drying, when po­ sitional crystal formation might be arbitrary. Particles are tightly bound together by strong hydrogen bonds and van der Waals forces once ice crystals have formed [138]. This is comparable to the method for pro­ ducing strong fibres that create uniform and fine threads from a little amount of CNF in water by combining hydrodynamic alignment with a sol-gel transition, but it takes a less complex approach [139]. In this case, however, the apparatus is simpler.
