The mechanical features of fracture, strain, and stress, as well as compressive, tensile, and shear moduli, are included in most reports describing mechanical properties of hydrogels [61]. Despite being widely used to depict the mechanical characteristics of hydrogels, these metrics fall short of giving a comprehensive, adequate description of hydrogels. On the other hand, fracture energy, a less often reported parameter, is a useful indicator of the suitability of certain hydrogels. This metric measures the energy needed to propagate cracks throughout the hydrogel's three-dimensional structure and is expressed as the energy needed to produce \(1\;\mathrm{J}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-2}\) of crack propagation [206,207]; This is a natural material characteristic that is unaffected by geometry or testing technique; it is thus valuable. For instance, one report [208] claims that the energy of bone fracture is \(12{-}73.2\;\mathrm{J}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-2}\); however, this
varies according on the orientation of the collagen inside the bone [209].
Breaking energy can be divided to (i) energy required to break through polymer chains that depends on density of chain in plane of crack path, number of bonds in those chains and strength of those bonds. Therefore hydrogels have lower energy threshold in hydrogel form as opposed to dry concentrated format of polymers \(10~\mathrm{J{\cdot}m^{-2}}\) vs 50–100 \(\mathbf{J}{\cdot}\mathbf{m}^{-2}\) [206,210]. (ii) Viscoelastic energy dissipated to surrounding network; therefore, like viscoelastic response defined in the literature is a property that is velocity (frequency) and temperature depending; therefore, type of loading static and dynamic affect the energy dissipation. Moreover, crack velocity and energy are correlated through a power law dependency; that is if crack is assumed to be propagating fast within the hydrogel it also requires higher amount of energy. Mechanism of dissipation in some polymers with dense polymer population is to dissipate energy through movement and rearrangement of polymer chains that is energy demanding due to close interactions between chains; however in hydrogel polymer is used in dilute form so polymers have very low number of neighbors thus energy dissipation is absent through polymer-polymer interactions thus fracture energy is low and hydrogel is not tough [206,210]. Therefore, increasing physical or chemical crosslinking can affect resiliency of hydrogel or aerogels. In order to generate reversible hydrogels with high fracture energy, Suo and colleagues [211] recently reported the production of interpenetrated calcium-alginate and covalently crosslinked polyacrylamide (PAM). The energy involved with fracture propagation must be dissipated with particularly effective dissipation. An enormous amount of fracture energy is absorbed by the embedded mechanism in the hydrogel, which dissipates by dissociating dynamic and reversible interaction. Though untested, it is intriguing that one new kind of cellulose, hairy cellulose [212], may have characteristics halfway between CNC and CNF. Understanding basics laid above can pave the way for formation of more resilient aerogels based on their pre-cursor hydrogel.
## 5.2. Advanced processing methods
A microfluidic setup, chemical crosslinking, 3-D printing, coagulation, and droplet creation can be used to further improve the processes already used. Adopting 3-D printing and microfluidics widely used techniques with CNF or CNC on their mix with other materials, can lead to production of value-added products, such as coatings, tissue scaffolds, thermal insulators, template materials, and so on with unprecedented properties. The method of aerogel production is not limited to 3-D printing or microfluidics techniques; however, these two techniques are required for creation of advanced product out of nanocellulose; moreover, due to rise in popularity of these techniques knowledge and their adoption is recommended for cellulose industry due to their potential mixing and processing in future with other materials with 3-D printing\additive manufacturing.
In the process of 3-D printing, material travels via a nozzle, escapes, and is then applied to a substrate. One of the key advantages of 3-D printing (4D printing refers to responsive\smart printing [213]) is to produce exceedingly complicated patterns or geometries that would otherwise be challenging to fabricate by hand [214]. Since most solvents cannot dissolve cellulose and it is non-meltable as CNC degrades in suspension at 90 and \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) [215], 3-D printing uses cellulose in a form (CNF and CNC) that exhibits shear thinning behaviour [15,216]; shear thinning results from particle orientation [93], which breaks their initial connections. Later, owing to Brownian movements, the particles return to their original positions, changing the viscosity before and after shearing. All varieties of CNF-CNC suspension have the property of shear thinning, which by definition implies that when the shear rate rises, the viscosity of the suspension descends [78]. Different levels of shear are required for different processing techniques; for example, shear rates equating to 1–1000, 100–1000, 100–10000, and \(10000{-}1000000\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) are required for common processing techniques like
pumping, extrusion, dispersion, and spraying [217]. Photonic crystals have previously been created using processes like as 3-D printing and spray coating [218] (the topic of the sections that follow); however, the literature [219] is sparse on the application of these methods to CNC suspension and CNC composites.
3-D printing has its own difficulties, such as die swelling after extrusion, hydrogel sagging, sedimentation, shape fidelity, nozzle clogging, and slip in nozzle are just tip of the iceberg. How accurately the 3-D printed structure follows the design intended is one of the main problems with 3-D printing; this problem is referred to in the literature as "shape fidelity". There are multitudes of avenues for tackling shape fidelity conformance. Chemically crosslinking the structure right after or during extrusion is one technique to solve this problem. Using chemical crosslinking, 3-D printed objects' structure can be preserved after extrusion; utilising CNCs that had been aldehyde- and hydrazide-modified, Yang et al. [44] created chemically cross-linked CNC aerogels that had consistent pore architectures and little structural collapse during drying; however, physically crosslinked hydrogels could undergo phase changes due to their more viscose nature therefore shape fidelity of chemically crosslinked sample expectedly were better than the physical version. CNC constructions created using the "Direct-InkWriting" strategy and with successful CNC alignments thanks to 3D printing have also been described in details by Studart et al. [123]; in order to improve shape fidelity compliance, CNC ordering in the nozzle is reversed during the rest phase (post extrusion). Using several feeding materials, a 3-D printed item may also be specifically transformed into a heterogeneous component. For example, the feed for certain components can switch between liquid crystalline, surface-modified, CNF, polymer, or other materials. These additional chemicals, such as thickeners (in the form of micro- and nanoparticles) [220], can control and increase shape fidelity by using functional nanofibril hydrogels and soluble cellulose.
Post-extrusion shape fidelity can also be helped by polymerization; this type of shape conformance assistance uses a chemical crosslinking technique found in nature. Due to the shear alignment of CNCs, different levels of orientation can be achieved in 3-D printed layers or strands; in some cases, maintaining shear within a zone that cannot disrupt the chiral structural integrity of the CNC can result in fringes of covalent bonds. 3-D printing has also been applied to pure CNC, CNC reinforced composites through direct injection of concentrated, viscoelastic aqueous or CNC gels imbued with monomer as well that later is planned to be polymerized [123]. The quality of 3-D printed objects is also enhanced by polymerizing the extruculate, particularly if this occurs shortly after extrusion. Polymerization after extrusion is closely connected to cryopolymerization, a procedure in which a monomer solution in a cryogenic or semi-frozen state is polymerized at subzero temperatures to create macroporous gels [221].
Die swell is another issue that arises during the extrusion of CNC pure or CNC composite hydrogels. For example, in ref [222], the filament width was measured as a function of material concentration; the material printed's mean standard diameter was \(1.81\pm15\ \mathrm{mm}\) , which was larger than the needle's diameter and suggested that die swell was occurring with the hydrogel. Die swell issue is intimately correlated with shape fidelity.
There are conventional polymers that had been used for 3-D printing include polyamide (PA) and acrylonitrile-butadiene-styrene (ABS) [223], which are petroleum-based 3-D material extrudates; however, given their widespread use and the strong incentives to reduce their use due to environmental concerns, more sustainable materials should be utilised instead such as cellulose products. However, in some application their addition into CNC gel can help with shape fidelity. The addition of polymer leaves shear thinning behaviour of CNCs, and CNFs unaffected due to shear thinning being physically stemmed in orientation of particles that remains unchanged when polymer is added (such as mixing with PVA) [15,216]. Shear thinning index however can change if polymer or another ingredient is mixed with CNCs [15].
The viscosity, which promotes a quicker hydrogel recovery after extrusion, is another factor supporting form integrity. As the CNC content [122] rises, so does the pressure required to print; however, higher viscosity also means quicker thixotropy and less sagging in the finished product. For instance, \(11.8\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) CNC constructs may hold their overall form right away after printing, but the relatively low viscosity gel often led to structural drooping after 5 to \(10\;\mathrm{min}\), according to the literature. Due to the CNC gel's \(11.8\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) liquid gel-like property, the sagging structure was endowed with greatly smoothed edge as well. Moreover, viscosity is adjustable with temperature and addition of thermos-responsive materials such as PNIPAM; heating and cooling to fasten healing process after and before 3-D printing.
Increasing content and adding polymer is not the only way to increase viscosity. For instance, for cases such as less concentrated CNF-filled materials; viscosity can be increased using thickening agents, including fumed silica [224], laponite [225], or high molecular weight polymers [226], to attain the optimum rheology for direct gel writing, whereas the intrinsic tangled up the state of concentrated CNF suspensions prevents high loading of CNF themselves. In ref [227], As the CNF content is decreased, a discernible loss in shape fidelity occurs. This is not the case when alginate is used in place of CNFs. Without losing form accuracy, these samples have a denser structure. These samples require a complex viscosity of roughly \(10^{4}\,\mathrm{Pa.s}\) at low angular frequency to retain good shape fidelity. This is found for composite gels made of CNFs and alginate as well as for pure CNF samples with a \(4\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) solid concentration.
The incapacity of cellulosic aerogel manufacturing to modify the creation of the aerogel structure can be solved by direct 3-D printing followed by coagulation (referred to as "regeneration" in the literature), resulting to the production of aerogel with no structural shrinkage or damage. Shape conformity is made easier by the physical crosslinking process known as coagulation. After the network of particles and polymer has passed the nozzle, the hydrogel must be vitrified by the addition of salt, which may be done by doping 3-D printing extrusion in a salt bath. The concentration of salt (or alternatively, ionic strength) in the bath can be used to determine how quickly vitrification takes place.
Due to its viscoelasticity, limited elastic characteristics, and susceptibility for structural deformations, printed filaments can exhibit creep and collapse, which is shown by the merging of the filaments or layers. This can happen if the yield stress (or storage modulus) is insufficient to overcome factors like gravity and surface tension, or if the gel does not stop flowing immediately after leaving the nozzle [64,228,229]. Gel collapse is closely associated with CNC concentration; as CNC concentration increases, gel collapse becomes of less of a concern [2,3]. Collapse of the pores following extrusion can change the shape of the generated aerogel. Ultimately, as the gel's thickness and CNC content rose, so did the print quality; this conclusion is consistent with other research on CNF-based 3-D printed hydrogels. To further improve printing quality, the \(400\;\mathrm{m}\) and \(200\;\mathrm{m}\) nozzle tip sizes were evaluated in addition to the \(500\;\mathrm{m}\) nozzle tip. As the nozzle tip size decreased, the connectivity of each layer from the \(\mathbf{Z}\)-directional surface became more continuously smoothed. However, as the nozzle tip resolution improved, so did the amount of time required to complete the print. In general, as printing resolution grew, print clarity and surface smoothness increased. It is also possible to change the procedure to make 3-D printing easier. In one study [230], TEMPO-CNF was printed in customised 3-D structures using a direct ink jet technique. The structures were then heated to crosslink the material, prolonging the hydrogels' usable life and lifetime.
There are various problems that can occur with a 3-D printing process in addition to form accuracy. Another significant problem that makes 3-D printing difficult for CNC and in particular CNF is clogging and the potential for clogging. In ref [231], the \(1.6\;\%\) CNF gel showed poor printability; it was difficult to maintain a continuous flow, and phase separation occurred in addition to tip blocking. By pushing the gel through a tiny nozzle under shear, flocculation was caused, and the blockage was attributed to this, or the probable existence of larger fiber
remains after fibrillation. To solve this problem, adding \(10{-}20\;\%\) skimmed milk powder or \(5\;\%\) starch to the gel did not prevent clogging; in fact, in the case of the latter, it made things worse; however, adding \(50\;\%\) of semi-skimmed milk powder (thickener) solved the printing issues because the material remained intact after printing. Designing the 3-D printing paste is undoubtedly a difficult task; typically, CNC has a lower clogging risk than CNF due to its shorter length.
The process of 3-D printing is further complicated by the existence of slip (apparent slide through the creation of a depletion layer) in addition to the issues [61–63]. Even before yielding and flow occurs in such colloidal systems, the interface gives, resulting in total slip (plug flow) of the systems [232,233]. By choosing the right grit size of sandpaper, the apparent "slip yield stress" that results from this may be avoided. When particle orientation is necessary, sandblasting must be done inside the nozzle because slip effects from generated plug flow might change the orientation of the particles after the nozzle. In instance, if the quantity of aerogel utilised in 3-D printing needs to be increased, the procedure must be scaled up. A variety of different cellulose sources can be used to create cellulosic aerogels. Aerogels are flexible enough to adapt to individual needs as required for a variety of applications, but one drawback is the challenge of customising aerogel 3D structures to allow for the rapid fabrication of objects with intricate geometry.
Fig. 4 displays that connection between rheological properties and gel being processed the schematic on top right shows strain amplitude test showing yielding point and flow point; schematic on the bottom right shows the hydrogel that can recover its original strength for different cycles of shear deformation.
Microfluidic setup and processing are another good setting, in addition to 3-D printing, for the creation of value-added products out of nanocellulose. Microfluidics is the science and technology of systems that are integrated channels on a microscale through which tiny volumes of liquid may flow and where the flow and the material within can be controlled or altered simultaneously [235,236]. The effects, such as the preponderance of viscous to inertial forces [237], the surface effects, and the increased mass and heat transfer [238], are magnified because the dimensions are tiny at the microfluidic scale, leading to behaviour that is different from that of macroscopic fluids. Aerogel made with the use of microfluidic technology differs from 3-D printed products; while 3-D printing aims to generate a precise object with controlled predefined dimensions, microfluidics aims to align particles, use microfluidics as mixing zones, or mass produce adsorbent aerogels with specified diameters [239]. The two technologies may both be utilised as bottom-up approaches to produce extremely complex designs, such as multilayer core-shell microcapsules or 3-D printed aerogel with perfect pore size and shape accuracy. The principal applications of microfluidic design are the production of cellulose fibres and beads, cellulose mixtures, and cellulose characterization. Due of this, although being unique, its use is more restricted to 3-D printing, which is widely used.
## 6. Applications
CNC experiences liquid crystal formation at a critical concentration that may be calculated using Onsager theory and aspect ratio [99]. This theory has been modified throughout time to take into account the effects of polydispersity [240,241], an external flow [242], and electrostatic repulsion [243]. The resulting structure is chiral because it enables the CNC to reflect various light wavelengths using the Bragg equation, giving the impression that the film is coloured. The development of these structures can be helpful for aerogels since a change in colour may indicate the need to use them as photonic crystals, humidity or temperature sensors, adsorbent materials, or in other applications. Other important properties like heat conduction and adsorption gain from the complex structure developed with autonomous self-assembly of CNCs.
## 6.1. Advanced porous structures
Advanced design of porous structure can happen if multitude of processing concepts and designs are taken into consideration. For instance, study [175] reports encasing metal organic framework (MOF) (a novel class of porous materials) powders inside a CNC based aerogel. As seen in Fig. 5a-e [175], the CNCs serve as a structural component to support the functional MOFs in a 3D flexible and lightweight networked material. Quantity and rate of adsorption of the aerogel has also been depicted in Fig. 5e; showing whether presence or absence of MOF can accelerate or alter final adsorbed quantity. MOF in addition to altering wetting behaviour of aerogels can also provide pores in meso range due to natural porosity of MOF therefore enhancing liquid uptake of aerogels. The loading of MOF similarly significantly improved the adsorption capacity of cellulosic aerogels for Congo red since MOF has abundant active sites in ref [244]. Similarly in another example; by crosslinking with hydrazide and surface-modifying CNC with aldehyde functionalization, low density and extremely porous aerogel was produced in ref. [44] as shown in Fig. 5-f-g crosslinked aerogel obviously can regain its shape more quickly after compression than physically cross-linked aerogel due to higher elasticity; however temperature, pH are less impactful on chemically crosslinked structures [61]; therefore physically crosslinked aerogels are more adapt at being used a responsive aerogels. Aerogel also displayed water absorption equal to \( 160 \pm 10 \, \mathrm{g/g} \) and a dodecane absorption of \( 72 \pm 5 \, \mathrm{g/g} \); therefore, aerogel was omniphilic. The literature pays close attention to enhancing the mechanical characteristics of nanocellulose aerogels since several cycles of adsorption are necessary for scalable and profitable aerogel manufacture and application.
The fragility of the carbon skeleton makes it challenging to achieve remarkable mechanical performance and recovery efficacy despite the substantial research on compressible carbon aerogels for oil/organic solvent recovery. In ref [245], a technique is suggested for producing a reduced graphene oxide (RGO) aerogel-based 3D stretchable crosslinked carbon. The carbon aerogel's strength and flexibility are both increased using PHL, an industrial by-product of plant pulping. The construction of wavy carbon layers with an elastic supporting microstructure enables mechanical stretch and shrinking as well as preventing interfacial collapse under compression [246]. The manufactured carbon aerogels are extremely light \( (4.98 \, \mathrm{mg/cm}^{3}) \) and exhibit supercompression (undergoing extreme strain of \( 95\% \)) and superelasticity (about \( 100\% \) height retention after 500 cycles at a strain of \( 50\% \)). These properties are made possible by the distinctive wavy layer structure and strong interaction between adjacent layers. In particular, the carbon aerogel exhibits significant oil/organic solvent absorption capacity (reaches up to \( 276 \, \mathrm{g/g} \) for carbon tetrachloride) and strong recyclability, allowing it to absorb diverse oily pollutants selectively and swiftly. This adaptable and durable functionalized carbon aerogel offers tremendous promise in the removal of oil and pollutants. See Fig. 5 h-j. The excellent dispersity and hydrophilicity of the GO nanosheets was confirmed with the Tyndall scattering effect (Fig. 5i). Because of the gaps between the sheets created by the arrangement in Fig. 5j, the resulting aerogels were less thermally conductive in a perpendicular direction; as a result, aerogels are anticipated to have uses other than adsorption as a result of their anisotropic heat conduction properties.
