When the compaction tension is applied direction perpendicular to the wave-like layers of the compacted and elastic carbon aerogel created by mixing chitosan and CNC in FeCl₃ aqueous solution, directional freeze-casting, and carbonization, aerogels are formed [247]. Because of the small material strain, the arch-shaped architecture can withstand a significant geometric deformation without collapsing [246]. The aerogel outperformed other compressible carbon materials such as CNT sponge [248], graphene aerogel [249], CNT-graphene aerogel [250,251], and so on in terms of fatigue resistance. After being continually compressed at \( 50\% \) for 60 days, there was no discernible height reduction (zero plastic strain).
Benefiting from molecular chain of cellulose and abundant hydrogen bonds between GO and CNC; GO nanosheets can be assembled with CNCs into layered structure during direction freeze-casting; however the strong adhesion between CNCs and GO layers weakens the nano support efficacy of CNCs and the interaction between neighboring carbon layers that ordinarily shows limited mechanical robustness [249]. The production of wavy carbon layers is guided by CNCs that is firmly attached to GO nanosheets. Additionally, low molecular weight dissolved xylosugar and lignin in PHL acted as an adhesion activator to improve the adhesion between carbon layers. Adsorption capability for different oils
and organic solvents ranged from 105 to \(276\,\mathrm{g/g}\) while strain as high as \(95\,\%\) was tolerated. Its adsorption capability exhibited no degradation even after ten cycles of adsorption and desorption.
## 6.2. Photonic crystals
LC are being used in non-display applications, and strong and enthusiastic study is being done to uncover its interesting features and occurrences (for instance aerogels). Recent advances in confined geometries, which are frequently shown as enclosed spherical and cylindrical geometries with an infinitely stretched axial-symmetrical space, are unusual [252]. In these disciplines, the interesting examples of liquid-crystal emulsion droplets and fibres have drawn a lot of attention [253]. The creation of sophisticated soft microstructures from both thermotropic and lyotropic LC has been made possible by the rapid development of microfluidics in addition to other novel techniques. In this section it is aimed to examine liquid crystalline formation of CNCs very briefly and its correlation with impact mechanical properties and its applicability within aerogels. LC can provide colour, serve as a framework for other nanomaterials or polymers to take shape, alter colour because of liquid penetration, and toughen aerogels when compressed.
Periodic structures with periodic variation in refractive index; with periods on the order of optical wavelength are called photonic crystals; CNC LC being an example of this as refractive index of CNC in longitudinal direction is different than transversal direction [4]. In nature, the
scales of the spotted parachute bird blue-skinned mandrill [254], Morpho butterfly wings [255], opals [256], and long-horned beetle [257] are examples of structural colours (colour creation from structure not photo adsorption with pigments or dyes); as a result, these structures are already present in nature. These structures have many 1-d, 2-d, and 3-d dimensions where the structure's periodicities are ordered.
Even though external forces (such as shear, electric, magnetic, etc.) can disrupt the organisation in order to align the CNCs in particular directions and obtain different material responses, the CNC chiral nematic organisation can act at long range and thus serve as a structural template for other material types of assembly [258] [259]. For example, shear-alignment of aqueous CNC suspensions has been investigated in the past to lower the coefficient of thermal expansion of nanostructured CNC films while maintaining film transparency [260]; this tendency is very advantageous in the design of organic electronic devices, especially light emitting diodes [261,262]. Having control over the coefficient of thermal expansion of nanocellulose based aerogel can be beneficial onto altering adsorption of aerogels. Molecular dynamics studies [263] show as opposed to graphene composites, CNC alignment may give low coefficients of thermal expansion while managing thermal conductivity and optical response, opening a wide range of design options. The thermal expansion and thermal conductivity of bulk cellulose \(\mathrm{I\beta}\) films are further influenced by elements including thermally triggered intercrystalline motion, crystal-crystal contacts, and crystal alignment.
## 6.2.1. Fundamentals and applications
Even though structural colours have exceptional optical properties, conventional structural materials only have partial control over the photon transmission channel. This can have a significant impact on the development of these materials as well as their use in photonic devices, integrated circuits [264] and colour encryption [265]. Additionally, because of their origin, which depends on how nanoparticles are arranged in space, photonic crystals change colour in response to factors such as wetness [266,267], temperature [268], acidity [269], electrical and magnetic cues [270], enzymes [271], alcohol [272], urea [273], and heavy metals presence [274].
Cholesteric LC, one of the many types of LC, are well-known due to their 1-d photonic crystals with a photonic band gap (PBG) due to their periodic helical structure [275]. Magnitude of LC pitch is one of its distinctive qualities. A helix is defined by its handedness; that is positive for right-handed and negative for counter-clockwise twist as well as its periodicity or wave number defined mathematically as \( q = \frac{2\pi}{p} \). All cellulose based fibrils are right handed according to microscopical observations [276]. Chirality can also be applied to individual particles; motion of electrons and molecules as well [277]; similar to self-similar fractal structures. Fig. 6 visually depicts the pitch and chiral structure of CNCs.
The wavelength of reflected light similar to solid crystals is described by wavelength of reflected light that causes colour creation [279]; the wavelength is determined by the pitch of the LC denoted through letter P in most publications as confirmed by reports [280,281] on CNC-based LC films (comparing applicability of Bragg law and observation of pitch length). The wavelength of reflected light for the periodic structures is according to Bragg equation [282,283] that is \( \lambda = nP \sin(\theta) \), where lambda is the reflected wavelength, P is the helical pitch, is the angle \( (^{\circ}) \) of the incident light, \( \sin(\theta) = 1 \) when the incident light is perpendicular to the crystal plane, and n is the average refractive index of the material [284]. Therefore, changing the pitch length and angle of view can result in reflected light having different wavelengths that fall within the spectrum of visible light, creating colour. These materials can adopt opalescent hues when the incident light wavelength is the same size and half the pitch sizes.
According to theoretical research on suspensions of rod- [285] and plate-like particles [286] restricted among two planar two walls (for example, slit-pore confinement), reasonably robust containment decreases the I N binodal; orientation imposed by the walls induces the creation of the N phase, which is now found at volume fractions for which the bulk is isotropic. Below a certain wall distance of particle
lengths, the capillary nematization phenomena return to normal. Another system of weakly shaped-anisotropic biaxial micellar aggregates contained the nematic phases between parallel walls. A phase transition results from wall anchoring, which reduces oscillations around the long particle axis and forces particles to align their shortest axis perpendicular to the wall [287]. It has been demonstrated empirically that the confinement [288–290] is altering the liquid crystal formation process when ordering into chiral nematic structures occurs sometimes in a small space with a spherical aerogel or hydrogel. It has not been investigated or well described by Onsager's hypothesis how confinement affects liquid crystal ordering and assembly [49,291]. In one report [252], the self-assembly of CNCs generated in an oil phase revealed that radial ordering took place throughout this process and that the created droplets after they were withdrawn from the system particle orientation retained their cholesteric, it was previously stated that liquid crystal arrangement in the most part after drying to limited mobility of particles retained their structure and knowing embedding particles within a droplet is a form of confinement.
By combining "ice-templating" and liquid crystal self-assembly techniques, pressure-responsive chiral photonic aerogels can be fabricated. The CNCs in the aerogels produced ribbons that support a secondary 3D cellular network through their primary 2D chiral nematic structured walls. Because aerogels are elastic in the solvent, pressure-induced rearrangement may easily convert their 3D structure to a 2D one. The colours of the aerogels range from white, which is caused by light scattering, to a reflecting photonic crystal, which, depending on the solvent in which it is dissolved, exhibits rich iridescent colours. A solvent-sensitive ink that responds swiftly to different solvents is made using the pressure-responsive photonic aerogel. This information highlights a unique method of action for the development of mechanoresponsive adsorbent aerogel and intelligent photonic systems.
Fig. 7 a–c shows typical CNC films for LC formed after evaporation with a locked-in chiral nematic helical structure. The arrangement of LC using scanning electron microscopy is depicted in Fig. 7c, making this characterization tool particularly flexible for identifying these evolutions. Fig. 7d shows a schematic of how a liquid crystal array is typically seen through an optical microscope lens, complete with fringes that provide the pitch length. Figs. 7e and 7f show photonic crystals' capacity to produce vibrant colour, which is comparable to the ability of some species of butterflies to produce colour naturally without the use of any chemical that interacts with light through reflection. Fig. 7e has been included to show that certain solidly coloured materials have an angel dependence, meaning that depending on the viewing angle, they exhibit a different colour. Fig. 7g displays the typical arrangement of LC in nematic ordering in space.
Scanning electron microscopy of the tiny particles, as shown in Fig. 7h-i, revealed that the cholesteric domain is still locally well-aligned to the severely crumpled surface (image of cross-section not shown here) [294]; An ordered state may be distinguished from an isotropic state using optical and scanning electron microscopy. Some researchers have attempted to harden the microstructure of CNC-water suspension to produce multifunctional optical materials. In addition to ensuring that the structure of photonic crystals becomes fixed, solidification after drying to achieve a glassy state [295,296] or combining hydrogel with salt before drying can control pith size while also causing the photonic crystal to exhibit vivid colour owing to selective reflection. Fig. 7j depicts an advancement in which, after CNC, chiral order has been imprinted within the silica aerogel.
The environment around CNC phases can also be polymerized as a way to lock in chiral nematic structure [297]; birefringent films have also been discovered in polar aprotic solvents like DMS [298]. Using (Hydroxyethyl)methacrylate (HEMA) polymer, this technology was used to produce films with iso, mix, and nematic ordering of CNC in addition to generating films in a variety of colours. End-grafting CNCs with other polymers in one report was shown to not affect liquid crystal formation; however, this observation might not be extendable to other
polymers or similar CNC shape changes [299]. In a similar study, a thermally "switchable" liquid-crystalline (LC) phase was found in CNC suspensions in water. CNCs with thermoresponsive polymer poly(N-isopropyl acrylamide) (PNIPAM) patchily grafted onto them. At room temperature, a suspension of the "patchy" PNIPAM-modified CNCs exhibits birefringence, demonstrating the existence of an LC phase. The birefringence vanishes at temperatures above the LCST of PNIPAM, signalling the entry of an isotropic phase [300].
By mixing a polymer with other polymers, such as PVA [301], that may interact with the liquid crystal form or maintain some wetness after film creation, variations in pitch size and therefore coloration can be produced. It is noteworthy [302] that the strength of the composite with LC characteristics outperformed all other samples examined. This is probably because more CNCs were used up during the phase separation of the original CNC suspension into iso and an-iso segments, which used up more CNCs with higher aspect ratios [302]. Furthermore, chiral structures are being researched to develop more robust materials that are once again inspired by nature; this may be the reason why films are made that not only have bright colours but are also difficult to break. Strong interactions between the host polymer and CNCs may cause the LC phase to disintegrate, as was the case with CNC and PVA cast films [301]; however, when fibres were involved, the ordering was visible in CNC\PVA fibres. In these circumstances, strategies like increasing CNC aggregation or adding compounds to reduce the affinity between CNCs
and PVA can be investigated. According to the author, sodium sulphate made the identical PVA-CNC dope pack together more cleanly even though it did not display LC behaviour or depend on mechanical strength in chiral nematic mesophase appearance [303]. The phase separation into an LC domain boosted the capacity of CNCS to strengthen fibres because they operate as a hard domain, according to the author's mechanical characteristics reasoning. Additionally, during the stretching of the fiber, the fibrillar mesophase of CNC might become orientated, increasing the elongation at break to greater values.
As previously mentioned, an external electric field or shear field may be used to align CNC particles and CNC liquid crystalline domains. There are, however, several different approaches of aligning CNC particles. Like how diamagnetic anisotropy-affected fibres align under static magnetic fields, cellulose fibres with the characteristic can experience magnetic alignment. Because a magnetic field may align the chiral nematic axis of CNC particles in the direction of the magnetic field, the orientation of CNC particles in conjunction with the gelation process may be altered to make extraordinarily strong CNC-produced fibres. Authors [304] reported using magnetic induced birefringence observation in dilute solution of CNCs under imposing high fields of magnetism (17.5 T). Aerogels that perform content adsorption and release on demand using an AC electric field or magnetic field can be created using changes in the microstructure of CNC in combination with the manufacturing of aerogels. Aerogel has been used with CNC to make
micropumps using spherical microcapsules with irregular shapes that could compress and pump using temperature; however, no research has been done to extend this capability to the magnetic field or AC field manipulation, and the same phenomenon has not been used to make actuator adsorbers that could tighten or release liquids from their pores due to temperature changes. Due to the negative diamagnetic characteristics of CNCs, it is possible for the orientation of layers following freeze casting or microfluidic spinning to change [296]. Using CNCs and polyethene glycol (PEG), spin-coating was employed to make self-assembling polyelectrolyte multilayer films (allylamine hydrochloride); interestingly; the samples at the center of spinning were isotropic however the CNC particles developed birefringence with thickness and the spin-coating method [305]. Moreover the pitch is changeable with temperature: PDMAEMA-grafted CNC aqueous suspensions were investigated at various temperatures with increasing temperature spacing of the fingerprint, pattern decreased [306].
Jamming [307] before formation of liquid crystalline phases and requirement for having a twist in CNCs can be the reason there is not enough sufficient report on formation of liquid crystalline phases with CNF [308]. When CNFs are disseminated in water at concentrations above the critical Onsager concentration [291], it has been discovered that they easily form a nematic lyotropic LC [309]. At infinitely small volume fractions of particles, the high length-to-width aspect ratio of CNFs (usually between 100 and 300) ensures the onset of nematic LC phase behaviour. Shortening can also help with formation of LC in CNF similar to CNTs [112]; however if shortening ensues CNF in actuality form CNCs. The correlation between persistence length and isotropic to nematic phase transition has to be explored to unlock the potentials of nanocellulose further in this area.
However, dried CNC films produce helical inner structures because of the colloidal structure of CNCs and their ability to self-order into LC. CNC may be moulded into a porous film using straightforward solvent casting as well [310]; The process may be broken down into many steps, each of which is controlled by temperature, the partial pressure of water in the air, and geometry; these elements combined govern how rapidly water vaporises. Onsager's theory states that the aspect ratio and concentration of CNC influence how it is split into LC domains [302,311]. As a result of the intricacy stated above, using cellulose as an aerogel with pre-defined characteristics is not a simple process.
## 6.3. Adsorbent aerogels
When it comes to aerogels made from nanocellulose, polymers can serve a variety of purposes, including: (i) enhancing flexibility; (ii) modifying hydrophobicity; (iii) enhancing processability; (iv) enhancing mechanical properties; (v) altering the propensity for liquid crystal formation; and (vi) making aerogels more chemically resilient. Polymerized CNF/micro fibrillates cellulose (MFC) aerogels with quick shape recovery capabilities by Zhang et al. [312] led to the formation of resilient aerogel, tolerating mechanical agitation and a hostile liquid atmosphere. This aerogel was made through the freeze-drying method; by solvent exchange using alcohol and ultra-critical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) drying, booth et al. [313] produced high porosity aerogels that were employable as filters, storage mediums, or catalytic support systems. Each method leads to porous aerogels however porosity is usually less through the freeze-drying method due to the collapse of some of the pores during water removal. This substrate is good for separation and extraction because of the porous nature of nanocellulose aerogel nanostructures, which enables rapid absorption of water and swell though the macropores and macropillary action of mesopores [175]. Macropores can also become mesopores with addition of nano sized fillers. In ref [314], a biocomposite aerogel was created by combining CNC/CNF freeze-drying with bleached cellulose fibres. Bleached fibres formed a 3-D network with thin coatings of CNFs, according to the material morphology. Comparing CNF to cellulose aerogel, a significant drop in pore size (6–13 nm, or within the meso-pore region) was simulated.
Any attempt to raise the chemical affinity between the solute and the adsorbent might enhance adsorption since adsorption is strongly related to it. One technique to enhance the affinity of aerogel toward non-polar components is modified nanocellulose. Because cellulose-based aerogels have a tendency to break down and scatter in water, chemical crosslinking, post-treatments such chemical vapour deposition (CVD), or other coating procedures must be utilised to create durable aerogels with increased liquid absorption [315,316]. CNF aerogels can handle water better than CNC due to their inherent entangled state, moreover due to their less crystallinity they seem to make more liquid than rigid CNC per unit of gram, Jiang and Hsieh reported CNF aerogels' potential amphiphilic adsorptive property by showing how well they could absorb 210 g/g of water and 375 g/g of chloroform [315,316]. These structures can take quite a bit of liquid because of their large surface areas, however for nonpolar solvents CNC aerogels have serious drawbacks, to deal with this problem, CNC must become more hydrophobic. In fact, owing to its ability for organic pollutant entrapment and oil/water isolation, the hydrophobization of CNF aerogels has attracted a lot of study interest [317,318]; these studies are easily extendable to CNC based aerogels as well. Results in the literature indicates that after hydrophobic modification of cellulose-based aerogels, their contract angle is usually >135° and their adsorption performance lies in range of 10–400 g/g a competitive figure to carbon and polymer based aerogels [319,320].
Materials for hydrophobic modification of CNCs mentioned in the literature are castor oil [321], polyphenols [322], butyric anhydride [323], grafting an organic acid chloride [97], grafting azetidinium salts [96] and so on. Zetidinium salts can be conjugated with sulfate groups of CNCs to make the surface hydrophobization of CNCs; this modification can render cellulose more thermally stable [324]. Many methods involve esterifying CNC, and most of these processes only alter the few hydroxyl groups that are located on the crystal surface's cellulose chains [7]. There have also been reports of CNC's esterification reactions with organic fatty acid chlorides of various sizes, however, the highest degree of substitution was <0.5 [325]. The internal hydroxyl groups of cellulosic clusters were unaffected by the gas-phase procedure that restricted esterification of cellulose to microfibrils [54].
Another approach is to make the aerogel mixed with hydrophobic polymer; in this cases nanocellulose acts as a reinforcer. For instance, in ref [168], elastic cellulose based aerogels with fast solvent adsorption, including water, were created utilising polyethyleneimine (PEI) and epoxy cross-linker. Chemically modified graphene sheets were added to the aerogel to make it a more selective oil adsorbent; as a result, the aerogel was able to adsorb organic liquids up to 58 g/g. Due to higher elasticity of these aerogels, after 100 cycles, mechanical characteristics remained unaltered. Similar to how aerogel can become omniphilic, in ref. [45] CNC was added to modified polysilsesquioxane (PSS), which was subsequently crosslinked with the CNCs during the sol-gel process. The same amount of polar (water) and non-polar (toluene) materials were adsorbable by these omniphilic aerogels. Similar research was conducted in ref. [326] to apply localised nitric oxide (NO) delivery by crosslinked aerogel for possible use in tissue engineering and related biological applications. In ref [327], the material was functionalized with methyltrimethoxysilane (MTMS) to enhance its hydrophobicity and oleophilicity. The aerogel achieved high absorption capacities of 18.4, 18.5, and 20.5 g/g for three different crude oils with high viscosity at 25 °C, respectively. Later adsorption was found to be temperature dependent as investigated temperature range of 10, 25, 40, and 60 °C for the absorption of the tested crude oil on the aerogel, achieved a higher absorption capacity of 24.4 g/g. Temperature likely reduced viscosity of penetrating liquid therefore increased soaking; moreover, due to expansion of aerogel with temperature, pores can also become larger. The reported adsorption capacities of these aerogels can be a better indicator of capacity if the reported values were given in addition to total available surface area, porosity, level of interconnectivity of pores in aerogel, geometry of pores and adsorbed liquid properties.
Surface hydrophobization can also take place with more
sophisticated protocols. For instance, one study attempted to graft elastic rubber onto CNCs to improve CNC aerogel's brittle nature and address the water sensitivity of CNC aerogels. With the introduction of rubber, the composites showed low density and shrinkage, high porosity and made the aerogel solvent resistant and had self-cleaning property [328]. After the creation of the CNC aerogel, CNCs were functionalized with a methacrylate group using reactions, and a variety of functionality was then imparted onto the aerogel by thiol-ene click chemistry; the modified aerogel had high adsorption of xylene [169].
Chemical vapour deposition (VD) has also been reported to promote hydrophobicity in CNC-PVA composite hydrogel [42,329]. Using this method, in one report, produced aerogels had a density ranging from 22.5 to \(36.1\;\mathrm{mg/cm}^{3}\), were highly porous \((>\!97.7\;\%)\), and could be utilised for 50 cycles without showing any signs of breaking or loss of mechanical strength [42]. Adsorption capacities for this aerogel was recorded for chloroform, hexane, acetone, ethanol, DMSO, mineral oil, pump oil, canola oil as 33, 25,22.5,25,30,20,27.5,22.5 g/g, respectively. Aerogel could be reused multiple times with slight reduction in efficiency due to estimated residual oil in the pores [330]. Moreover being 30 times less dense that water, the aerogels could float on top of the water; making it a perfect substrate for tackling oil spills that usually sit on top of the water as well.
More effortlessly, without the need for chemical treatment, aerogel with strong amphiphilic adsorption ability could also be created by mixing simple aerogel components like graphene and CNC [331,332]. The adsorption of diclofenac sodium (DCF) was also tested this time using polyvinyl amine (PVAm) and rGO aerogel [177]; the adsorption capacity increased as a result of the modification by 60 fold when compared to bare CNC aerogel; in contrast to previous examples, cycles for adsorption and desorption could only be repeated four times. In addition to having a high capacity for absorption, optimal absorbent materials should also be extremely hydrophobic and oleophilic to achieve high efficacy in the selective removal of oil from water. Parallel to this, Wu et al. [333] created a carbon nanofiber aerogel by pyrolyzing bacterial cellulose and attained a water contact angle of \(110{-}130^{\circ}\) and an absorption capacity of \(100{-}300\;\mathrm{~g/g}\). In a similar report [334], omniphilic aerogel capable of adsorbing both water and hexane equal \((\sim\!12\;\mathrm{g/g})\) had apparent contact angle of \(106^{\circ}\). These aerogel have apparent contact angle as droplet place on them due to them being highly porous adsorb the droplet due to capillary action [334].
Other porous materials can also be entered by CNCs to produce hydrophobic aerogels. According to one study, CNC coated the surface of melamine sponges after entering them, and when it was pyrolyzed, it had a high capacity to absorb oil [335]. In one work [336], flexible aerogel produced with CNC and CNF using a sol-gel and freeze-drying technique was blended with copper benzene tricarboxylate (Cu-BTC). At \(39\;\mathrm{mg/g}\) for Congo Red (largely hydrophobic), the Cu-BTC/NFC aerogel composite exhibits good adsorption. CNC aerogel, however, exhibited no adsorption of Congo Red. By converting permanganate ions into manganese dioxide, CNC aerogel and Cu-BTC/NFC were both able to remove permanganate ions from water without releasing any additional products into the solution. Cu3(BTC)2 (BTC = 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate) is a microporous MOF with three-dimensional cubic network wherein Cu (II) dimers are linked into paddlewheels by BTC. This MOF, first reported by Chui et al. [337], is now one of the most studied MOFs.
Surfaces with a hierarchical structure have demonstrated outstanding water repellency [338,339]. In reality, nature offers the very earliest instances of superhydrophobic surfaces, including the nonwetting legs of water strides and lotus leaves [340–341]. To attain high water repellency, synthetic microtextured surface patterns resembling lotus leaves have been created. On these surfaces, water droplets are often in the Cassie state [342] rather than the Wenzel state [343]. For instance, adding a second layer structure on micropillars gave the textured surface superhydrophobicity [344]. The volume of the air pocket increases in conjunction with this development [345]. The
primary cause is the rise in solid-liquid-gas composite interfaces because of hierarchical architectures developed deep within aerogel. Using these hierarchical structure Gao et al. [338] reported fabrication a structure with a low-surface-energy covering and an ordered graphene-based hierarchical structure combined to create robust superhydrophobic foam. This foam exhibits super-hydrophobicity in both air and oil (160.1° and 171.0°, respectively), as well as strong resilience to repeated abrasion cycles. It can readily separate oil from water with great efficiency and strong recyclability due to its multiple superhydrophobic surfaces and 3D porous architectures; these aerogels are therefore very adapted at clearing out emulsions and fouling on membranes as well. Droplets often display one of different states on rough hydrophobic surfaces (similar to modified cellulose-based aerogels with pore spaces: the Cassie state [342] in which water droplets are in contact with the peaks of the rugged surface as well as the "air pockets" confined in surfaces ridges, or the Wenzel state [343] in which water droplets are in complete contact with the rugged surface (referred to as the wetted contact) (the composite contact). The thickness of the nanopillars, the distance between them, the inherent interfacial adhesion, and the infringing velocity of the water nanodroplet ("raining" simulation) are physical factors that can significantly influence the transition. Beyond a certain pillar height, water droplets on the pillared surface can either be in the "Wenzel" or the "Cassie state", depending on their initial location.
In addition to assisting with the processing and adsorption of aerogels, polymers can also enhance their reusability by enhancing their mechanical properties; in the field of polymer science, products are routinely put to the "fatigue test," which is a test that can qualitatively ensure long-term usage of a product. The reusability of aerogel in nature is similar to the "fatigue test." Aerogel applications in literature made with CNC [44,179] and CNFs [151,166] are occasionally strengthened with a water-soluble polymer, like PVA, to address their brittleness. PVA hydrogel is one example of an aerogel that has had CNC or CNF added to it to improve it; moreover, PVA is benign therefore mixing it with CNC or CNF can still produce an aerogel safe for water treatment. Gelation can occur through variety of methods of cellulose products; however it can simply be induced through numerous freeze-thaw cycles [346]. Doing so would greatly improve efficiency and also lower the cost [347].
In one report, methylene blue (MB) after 5 cycles of use showed a slight decrease in adsorption (initial adsorption 116 mg/g) in CNC/poly (methyl vinyl ether-co-maleic acid)/poly (ethylene glycol) composites aerogel. The aerogel's negatively charged compounds from poly (methyl vinyl ether-co-maleic acid) and sulfonate groups from CNCs offered places where cationic MB dye molecules could be electrostatically attracted to the aerogel. The portion of preabsorbed MB particles retained in the aerogel structure, which lowered the total amount of accessible negatively charged carboxyl groups for future sorption cycles, may be the cause of a modest decline in MB adsorption performance [146]. The adsorption of methylene blue (a cation dye) can further increase if GO is amalgamated with the aerogel as shown in ref [348] due allowing further active sites for adsorption. Additionally, it is anticipated that CNC-supporting technologies will be closer to the aerogel's surface than they would be buried deep inside it, and that CNCs with larger charges will be able to achieve higher adsorption values.
Another work used CNCs to create a light, elastic carbon aerogel that addressed mechanical property improvement by using carbon nanotubes with a similar structural make-up. The samples were afterwards pyrolyzed; the word "pyrlozed" is also used to refer to carbonization in literature. Carbon aerogels (cellulose pyrolysis product) are made by carbonising cellulose in an environment of nitrogen or argon and heating the aerogel to a temperature of 500–1000 °C. Konjac glucomannan (KGM) linked CNCs together to form continuous, orientally oriented layers with a wavy shape. CNCs functioned as the structural unit. Even after 10,000 cycles at 50 % compression strain, aerogel maintained its height at 100 % and its stress at 90% [349]. KGM is composed of linear β-1,4 linked D-mannose as the backbone, but with 1,6-linked α-D-galactopyranose and β-D-glucopyranose units
respectively as the side chains [350]. In an application targeted at adsorption of gas instead of liquid, CNCs were created by grafting CNC hydrogel with N-(2-aminoethyl) (3-amino-propyl) methyldimethoxyanisole. The amount of CO2 adsorbed by the modified aerogel at a pressure of 3 bar \((2.63\;\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{g})\) was much higher than that by the unmodified aerogel \((0.26\;\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{g})\) [351]. Similarly for GO, ref [352]; the hierarchical lamellae-bridge structure of the reported scalable carbon aerogel, which was inspired by the structure of the T. dealbata plant, used GO and KGM with a bidirectional freeze casting approach. The unique layered structure of the aerogel made it not only extremely low dense at \(4.2\;\mathrm{kg}/\mathrm{m}^{3}\), but also extremely durable with 1000 strain recyclability.
Using a number of ways, researchers cross-link CNC aerogel to improve its water repellent qualities, render it impervious to invading water molecules, or stop it from breaking owing to the compression and release cycles needed for an adsorbent [179]. Although untreated (by crosslinking or functionalization) CNF pure aerogels are still vulnerable to water molecule invasion, entanglements make these aerogels more resistant than CNCs [353]. Despite the fact that CNC clusters (CNF may be comparable, but has not yet been tested) were proven to be resilient to the temperature fluctuation of water molecules [2], the link between CNC clusters owing to swelling may break. Furthermore, the diffusion experiment shows that these clusters are fixed, and confocal laser scanning microscopy essentially is unable to detect any movement [204]. Although physical aggregation (or crosslinking) can hinder self-healing [204], research hasn't yet attempted to establish a correlation between CNC hydrogel or aerogel fracture energy and the degree of chemical or physical crosslinking.
There are also ways to surface-modify cellulosic particles, such using surfactants that can surface-modify and gelate CNCs [1]. Although it has been seen that these observations affect the rheological and gelation characteristics, no further steps have been taken to put these recent discoveries to use in actual applications. Clean suspensions of CNF demonstrated strong shear thinning behaviour, dependency on storage modulus on CNF power law dependence, yield stress fluid in terms of rheology, and yield stress fluid. The solution became extremely unstable when cationic surfactants were introduced; however, non-ionic surfactants made the suspension stable up to an \(8\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) concentration. Although sodium laureth sulphate (SLES) was as stable as non-ionic surfactants [354], sodium dodecyl sulphate (SDS) also induced gelation in anionic surfactants. Inductive tests are utilised to ascertain how surfactants alter colloidal behaviour because surfactant type affects hydrophobization. Because it is more likely to be washed away with non-polar liquid being adsorbed throughout cycles, the surface modification method of changing aerogel hydrophobicity using surfactants is less advantageous than other surface modification methods.
Man-made polymer oil sorbents, such as polypropylene fibres, polyurethane foams [355], and polypropylene nonwoven webs [356], are commercially available for cleaning up oil spills due to their inherent oleophilicity and hydrophobicity; however, these materials are not sustainable and degrade very slowly in the environment. In research in ref [357], Waste cellulose fibres underwent nanofibrillation and hydrophobic modification (silylation), resulting in nanofibril sponges that were extraordinarily porous (up to \(99.81\;\%\) ) and had a very low density \((0.0029\;\mathrm{g/cm}^{3})\). These sponges could be reused and were excellent at absorbing various oils and organic solvents. Particularly the nanofibril aerogels showed selectivity in the absorption of marine diesel oil from an oil-water combination. Due to the low densities of the cross-linked aerogel and high porosity, 3-D printed aerogels can be superabsorbent and have petroleum separation capabilities.
The yield stress and elastic modulus of cellulose-based cellular structures typically range between 10 and \(100\;\mathrm{kPa}\) [46,358], despite the fact that CNFs' elastic modulus reaches 150 GPa [359]. As a result, creating a cellular structure made of cellulose that is both light and powerful is feasible but still calls for further research. In ref [360], a lightweight and incredibly durable honey comb structure made of
cellulose hydrogel was built with the help of 3-D printing. According to contemporary structural analysis approaches, nature has cellulose-based honeycomb structures; there are several biological examples with cellular and light structures that are mechanically strong and enduring in nature. In this regard, cork is a notable example because of the crucial role played by its efficient hierarchical structural assembly in ensuring the material's elasticity, high specific mechanical strength, and light weight. For instance, chewed wood fibres combined with saliva are used to construct paper wasp nests [360]; therefore production of these hydrogels are inspired by nature (please see the development of this aerogel in Fig. 8 a-d).
The printed structure could be reconstituted in a water bath and freeze dried to obtain the final aerogel form [360], and Fig. 8a showed a 3-D printed structure with excellent structural integrity and form accuracy. After regeneration, it was found that the 3-D cellulose honeycomb sheet was flexible and could be twisted, folded, and twisted in any way. Without losing its capacity to keep its original rectangular shape, the honeycomb sheet may also be twisted into a bowtie shape (see Fig. 8a). For applications where the aerogel must dynamically absorb and desorb oil or other non-polar liquids, these macro assembly structures can further fortify aerogels. The yield stress values for 3, 4, 5, and \(6\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) cellulose in wet state is \(19\pm1\), \(33\pm2\), \(65\pm1\), and \(91\pm3\;\mathrm{Pa}\); the low yield stress values imply ease of processing. The corresponding Young's moduli for 4, 5, and \(6\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) were however \(9.5\pm3.2\), \(16.6\pm2.6\), and \(11.1\pm2.2\;\mathrm{MPa}\). The freeze-dried honeycomb structure's Young's modulus, which is around two orders of magnitude larger than that of the analogous wet structures, supports the strong connection between cellulose chains. The reason for the decreased mechanical properties at \(6\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) cellulose concentration is the higher ratio of undissolved cellulose, which may prevent hydrogen bonding and intimate association between cellulose chains and as a result lead to a more porous cell wall with reduced mechanical performance. Due to its strong mechanical qualities and porous structure, the 3D-printed cellulose honeycomb may be used as a thermal insulation panel. If the hysteresis of the aerogel throughout cycles is examined to show how much of the stress is dissipated during cycles, the results of these might be further examined.
In Fig. 8e-h, 3-D printing process for CNC gel is depicted [122]; This result agrees with past studies on CNF-based 3-D printed hydrogels, where the print quality increased as the gel's thickness and CNC content increased. In addition to the \(500\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) nozzle tip, the \(400\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) and \(200\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) nozzle tip sizes were assessed to further enhance printing quality. The connection of each layer from the z-directional surface became more continuously smoothed as the nozzle tip size shrank. The time needed to finish the print increased as the nozzle tip resolution grew, though. In general, print clarity and surface smoothness improved as printing resolution climbed. In ref. [361], the best shape fidelity for hydrogel printing has been observed to be between 100 and \(200\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\), which is among the highest resolution levels currently possible with hydrogel inks [362]. The diameter of the needle had a significant impact on this resolution, with adoption of narrower tips leading to better resolution similar to ref [122].
In Fig. 8i-k, TEMPO-CNF was printed in customised 3-D structures using a direct ink jet technique. The structures were then heated to crosslink the material, prolonging the hydrogels' usable life and lifetime [230]. When TEMPO-CNF and CNF viscosities were compared during the homogenization process, it was discovered that the CNF viscosity trend continued increasing with homogenization while the TEMPO-CNF trend increased initially before declining. The decline in viscosity is associated with the removal of repulsion interactions between CNF fibres caused by the negative charges of TEMPO-CNF after five homogenization phases. The author also notes that viscosity of the gels appeared to be the main element in their ability to maintain their form after printing. Later, shape recovery of water saturated TEMPO-CNF aerogels with different values of compression were evaluated with dynamic mechanical analysis as shown in Fig. 8j. The form recovery % decreased as the number of compression-release cycles increased, indicating a
modest irreversibility of the compressions, although the amount of recovered aerogel was still \(>90\;\;\%\) with an \(80\;\;\%\) compression rate. Because the loading curves at gradually higher compression amounts were almost overlapping with one other, the resulting stress–strain curve indicated that the compressibility of TEMPO–CNF aerogel is extremely reversible. However, there was hysteresis between the loading and unloading curves, indicating that dissipative energy was lost. The well-known Mullins effect [363,364] which characterises the stress-softening behaviour of rubber-like materials, is distinct from the stress-strain behaviour. The key variation is in the succeeding reloading cycles, when the reloading curves adhere to the initial primary loading route rather than the unloading path from the prior cycle; this difference is due to reformation of reversible hydrogen bonds during 1 min rests between cycles. Aerogel made with TEMPO-CNF also shows high uptake of water at levels of \(35~\mathrm{g/g}\) due to swelling of amorphous CNFs in the aerogel. It was determined that the potential water absorption capacity was \(35~\mathrm{g/g}\). The fact that only \(92\;\%\) of the predicted water absorption capacity was determined experimentally leads to the conclusion that about \(8\;\%\) of the pore volumes were inaccessible to water. Before CVD, water and decane absorption capacities of the hydrophilic aerogel were about 35 and \(8~\mathrm{g/g}\), respectively; after CVD this number changed to 1 and \(4~\mathrm{g/g}\), respectively; therefore, using a CVD technique with 1H,1H,2H,2H perfluorooctyl-trichlorosilane (PFOTS), it is feasible to transform the 3D printed TEMPO-CNF aerogels into a highly water non wettable and water nonadsorbing aerogel.
## 6.3.1. Reusability and adsorption
Aerogel is known to need to be reused for adsorption purposes; the cycles mentioned in the literature are: \(225\,\mathrm{g/g}\) adsorption of MB for after one cycle of usage capacity was reduced by approximately \(86.2\,\%\) of the initial capacity, remaining almost constant in the five subsequent cycles for CNC-carboxymethylated Chitosan [365]; reduction of adsorption capacity from \(14\,\mathrm{g/g}\) for ethanol and pump oil to \(12\,\mathrm{g/g}\) and \(10\,\mathrm{g/g}\) after 15 cycles of use cellulose aerogel reinforced with CNCs [366]. The decrease in adsorption capacity may be caused by cycles that destroy the aerogel's structure or by oil residue that remains in the pores and lowers the aerogel's capacity.
In one study [367], the computed pore volume per gramme of hydrophobic CNF aerogel was \(42.4\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}\), supporting the hypothesis that the oil removal capacity of CNC aerogel is exclusively determined by the overall volume of aerogel. A minimum of \(16\,\%\) of the pore volume was inaccessible to liquid, most likely owing to blocked or restricted interconnectivity of \(16\,\%\) of the pores, according to sketches of the varied adsorption capacities of various liquids that aerogel might fill up those pores till capacity of \(73{-}84\;\%\) pore volume. Pore volume accessibility was further reduced by \(11\;\%\) for higher density liquids like dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) and chloroform. These findings imply that tight confinement of liquid and strong structural strength against pore deformation need tiny holes with relatively thick walls, which are crucial geometrical factors. In the case of reusability, structural deformation of aerogels occurred during liquid evaporation at room
temperature in the first cycle as macropores irreversibly contracted, resulting in decreased pore volume. For the following repetition cycle, the dried aerogels' reduced pore capacity results in less oil absorption [367]. The maximum absorption capacity, however, was not decreased after a second repeat cycle as no further structural deformation was observed; this observation was similar to ref. [365]. Moreover, the adsorption capacities for toluene with density of \(0.863\;\mathrm{g/mL}\) and chloroform with density of \(1.49\;\mathrm{g/mL}\) were 30.0 and \(46.6\;\mathrm{g/g}\), respectively; these values were similar to previously reported values for hydrophobic nanocellulose aerogels [317,368]. The modified CNF aerogel structure
may also partially swell when exposed to solvents with high polarity and moderate surface energy, such as pyridine and DMSO. Swollen cellulose has a bigger total pore capacity, which can result in more liquid penetration. A lot may be deduced about the hidden porous structures in aerogel from changing liquid and the measured adsorption capacity.
The unmodified micro-fibrillated cellulose aerogel in ref [369] had hydrophilic property and could absorb water droplet immediately; in contrast, the modified version was hydrophobic with a contact angle up to \(151.8^{\circ}\) higher than reported values for hydrophobic cellulose materials. Aerogel wettability is another metric that can determine
adsorption capacity and aerogel reusability [316,333,368,370–372]. Another crucial problem that needs more investigation is the question of how much apparent contact angle affects aerogel's omniphilicity.
## 6.3.2. Morphology
In addition to porosity, the gel's morphology also provides information on potential characteristics of nanocellulose aerogels; the morphology can alter depending on the way the surface is modified, if nanofillers are added, what kind of nanocellulose is used, and the concentration. For instance, a study [381] discovered that the CNC-Aerogel has a spherical morphology, but the CNF-Aerogel has a rice-shaped morphology owing to the longer CNF filament, making it more prone to drag and drop. So, the more CNF added to the mixed aerogel, the appearance of the aerogel was more like a rice-shape [31]; the aerogel made using solvent exchange with tert-butyl alcohol. In ref [373], it was shown that through controlling composition of CNC\poly(oligoethylene glycol methacrylate) (POEGAM) dispersion and tunning freeze casting temperature aerogels with fibrillar, columnar and lamellar morphology could be made. Freeze casting temperature varied between \(-80\) and \(-20\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The aerogel freeze-casted at \(-20\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) formed a more well-defined lamellar-like structure, in comparison with the same sample prepared at \(-80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Typically, at lower freezing velocities achieved at smaller temperature gradients, CNCs and POEGMA had sufficient time to be excluded from the growing ice crystals, thus yielding more organized structures [373]. Similarly for graphene-based aerogels casting at \(-196\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) GO aerogels were developed with small cellular pores \((10\,\upmu\mathrm{m})\) and thin pore walls \((20\,\mathrm{nm})\) while casting at \(-10\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) led to formation of larger pores \((800\,\upmu\mathrm{m})\) and thicker pore walls \((80\,\upmu\mathrm{m})\) [374]. Water, camphene, camphor-naphthalene, and tert-butyl alcohol are the four most often used solvents in freeze-casting; each produces a specific pore architecture, such as lamellar, cellular, dendritic, and prismatic, respectively [136]. The ice crystals form in the vapour phase as dendritic (snowflakes), plates, needles, hollow columns (finger like), smoothed plates, or faceted structures, in accordance with the Gibbs-Curie-Wulff Theorem [375,376]; example of such structures are shown as a function of supersaturation and temperature in Fig. 9a. Fig. 9b illustrates the shape and lightness of potential aerogel forms. As previously mentioned, aerogel may also take the form of microbeads, films, irregular shapes, and 3-D printed structures with a variety of functions and complexities.
Similar findings have been found with silica aerogels [381], where scientists described microporous ceramics that were shaped by unidirectional columnar ice crystals and in which the pore width was negatively associated with the cooling rate. Fig. 9c-e shows the general process followed to create an aerogel based on nanocellulose products; in 9c, the cooling gradient is 1-D, in 9d, it is bidirectional, leading to the orientation of the macropores shown, and in 9e, the cooling gradient is radial, resulting in various macropore formations within the aerogel. The micro-structural gradient develops across the thickness of the aerogel if the temperature is not low enough. Therefore, it is required to choose a lower temperature for the freezing bed to obtain directed porosity. The porosity in this instance has a low tortuosity, which is advantageous for a gas transmission medium and scaffolding nerve cell [49]. The macropores created by the sublimation of ice crystals may also produce smaller scale porosities if mixed with other materials. These materials, which are inherently micro or mesporous and can create smaller scale porosities, such as other smaller colloids [382], organic micelles [383], and metal-organic framework materials [384] can be added to nanocellulose aerogel.
Freezing can be repeated many times to alter morphology of aerogels; after multiple cycles, lamellar or honeycomb structures can be created; in fact, the ice growth and phase separation are improved by the repeated cycles thus further altering morphology colloids confined within growing crystals. In ref [315], for TEMPO-CNF aerogel; the density more than doubled after 7 freeze thaw cycles; the pore volume decreased from \(99.5\,\%\) to \(98.8\,\%\); and an ultra low dense aerogel was made. The ultra low density of the aerogel at \(8\,\mathrm{mg/cm}^{3}\) was
approximately equal to carbon nanotube sponges \(5{-}10\,\mathrm{mg/cm}^{3}\) [385–386] and it was only two to nearly three fold times the lightest carbon nanotubes aerogel \((4\,\mathrm{mg/cm}^{3})\) [387] and silica \((2{-}3\,\mathrm{mg/cm}^{3})\) [388] ever reported. Similarly for lignocellulose aerogel [389] after 6 cycles; aerogel exhibited the lowest density as the 3-D network became more compact. Temperature of freeze thaw cycles is also important, when CNF suspensions are frozen at \(-20\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), slower super-cooling of the suspensions can concentrate CNFs into more precisely assembled crystalline domains, and slower ice crystal nucleation can grow, leaving pores that are \({>}20\) times larger than those left by rapid super-cooling at \(-196\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). When CNF is commonly frozen in liquid nitrogen \((-196\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\), many small ice crystals are formed. These crystals prevent CNF from associating or gelling and, when freeze-dried, create aerogels with smaller pores of 10 to \(30\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) that have very weak structural integrity and little water absorption.
The alteration of the surface of aerogel building block is yet another factor that might alter morphology of aerogels. In ref [159], morphology images of the CNC aerogel are displayed both before and after chemical vapour deposition (CVD) modification. The original ellipsoid shape of the modified CNC aerogel was kept, but its colour altered from white to a yellowish tint. At low magnification, the interaction of two-dimensional lamellar layers produced a porous structure in the amine-modified CNC aerogel; at greater magnification, the network generated seemed denser in comparison to unmodified aerogel; these macro-porous structures mostly mirrored the internal structure of the CNC aerogel before modification and were left by the rising ice crystal column during the freezing process [159].
Concentration also affects morphology of aerogels, in ref [390], by adjusting the sol-gel concentration, porosity was easily managed. The underlying substrate was made visible by the sparse and poorly linked aerogel network, which had a weight percentage of \(0.3\,\mathrm{wt\%}\). When compared to 1 or \(3\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) aerogel films, \(0.3\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) aerogel films seemed uniform, yet noticeably more transparent. As anticipated, utilising larger gel concentrations caused a drop in the average pore diameter seen in the photos. The surface of the all-CNC aerogel showed densely packed sheet-like structures at \(3\,\mathrm{wt\%}\). Developing ice crystals compress the adjacent net into clumps at a certain threshold of gel content and freezing kinetics, which is consistent with observations from previous studies [44,180]. When freezing starts close to the substrate in contact with the freezing solution, smaller ice crystals and subsequently smaller holes are created. On the top of the film, ice crystals grow larger and aggregates that resemble sheets as it freezes more slowly. The distinctive lamellar-like structure of the CNC sheets, which is parallel to the direction of ice crystal formation and is consistent with past findings, supports this hypothesis. Additionally, it has been demonstrated that the size of the GO precursor and the concentration of the initial GO solution have an impact on the pore size of other GO-based aerogels [391,392].
Table 6 lists various absorbents created recently along with their capacity for absorption of nonpolar liquids. The cellulosic aerogels' absorbance capabilities in one ref. [369] were considerably higher than those of other known absorbents made of synthetic polymers \((14{-}57\,\mathrm{g/g})\) [393], \(20{-}50\,\mathrm{g/g}\) of cellulose fibres [394], absorbents made of nanocellulose \((20{-}185\,\mathrm{g/g})\) [151,367,368,370,371], chitin sponges [395], cellulose-based carbon aerogels \((29{-}192\,\mathrm{g/g})\) [396–398], and silica aerogels \((16\,\mathrm{g/g})\) [399]; however, they weren't as high as those for graphene and carbon nanotube aerogels, which displayed absorption capacities as high as \(200{-}743\,\mathrm{g/g}\) [385,400]. Additionally, the commercial viability of graphene and carbon nanotube aerogels is constrained by the price and complexity of the manufacturing procedures. The adsorption capacities shown in Table 6 are intriguing because the higher surface area and number of functional groups in carbon aerogels generally result in higher adsorption capacities; an attempt to pyrolyze cellulose aerogel or the combination of cellulose and these fillers is predicted to result in higher adsorption capacities for cellulose-based aerogels. As shown in ref [369], adsorption capacity increases with density of oil and non-polar liquids, with chloroform \((260\,\mathrm{g/g})\) showing
the highest adsorption capacity compared to hexane \((120~\mathrm{g/g)}\) , as the density of hexane is approximately \(0.665~\mathrm{g/cm}^{3}\) while the density of chloroform is \(1.49~\mathrm{g/cm}^{3}\) ; however in terms of volume of adsorbed liquid chloroform is adsorbed at value of \(175~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\) while hexane at 181 \(\mathrm{cm}^{3}\) . According to the literature, denser liquids result in larger \(\mathbf{g}/\mathbf{g}\) re­ ports, hence the quantity specified should be reported together with the viscosity and density of the liquid that has been adsorbed onto the aerogel for depiction of a fuller picture of aerogel ability.
Volume-based absorption capacities of the cellulose aerogels might reach up to \(90\ \%\) , indicating that almost all their pores were clogged with organic solvents or oils. The cellulose aerogels' capacity to absorb oil on a volume basis was somewhat less than that of other organic solvents because of the high viscosity of the oils. Approximately 25 s are needed for cellulose aerogels to reach absorption equilibrium in mineral and motor oils, although this time is shortened in other organic solvents [369]. A liquid with a greater viscosity than a liquid with a lower viscosity takes longer to reach absorption equilibrium because the viscosity of a liquid greatly impacts the speed at which its molecules move.
Following ten cycles of absorption and desorption, pump oil had an
absorbency of \(151~\mathrm{g/g}\) , which became \(98~\%\) of its original uptake ca­ pacity. Nanocellulose's contact area with water decreased somewhat as well, from \(151.8^{\circ}\) to \(149.6^{\circ}\) . The cellulose aerogels still displayed good hydrophobicity \((124.9^{\circ})\) and a high capacity for absorption \((92\,\mathrm{g/g}\) for pump oil) following 30 cycles of absorption and desorption, highlighting their extraordinary reusability. Researchers were able to gauge the robustness of the aerogel by syphoning the samples with different sol­ vents. The aerogel' water contact angle decreased from \(151.8^{\circ}\) to around \(120^{\circ}\) because of the selective removal of polysiloxane particles from the aerogel surface during the leaching studies. The lowering of motor oil and pump oil's absorption capacities from 158 to about \(115\,\mathrm{g/g}\) and 154 to roughly \(110\ \ \mathrm{g/g}\) , respectively, shows the aerogel 'exceptional endurance in practical applications.
## 7. Conclusions and projections for the future
using layer-by-layer self-assembly and nanoscale pore-forming technologies to create electrolyte materials and synapses for bioelectronics. According to Cassie-Baxter theory [342], to improve wettability, surface energy can be decreased, re-entrant surface textures increased, and hierarchical structures can be built. While the other two seek to increase the proportion of the liquid-gas interface, frequently by forming "air pockets," the first technique helps to increase the material's intrinsic contact angle. Given this, coatings with low surface energy groups like the fluoromethyl (-CF3) and methyl group (-CH3) should work well to create superhydrophobic materials when surface roughening is also used. The literature currently lacks thorough study of these changes in the adsorption behaviour of nanocellulose-based aerogels, and this has a significant and potentially game-changing effect on the development of these hydrogels.
It's also fascinating how photonic crystals are used in cellulose aerogels. When created with LC phase CNCs and freeze drying, pressure-responsive chiral photonic aerogels may produce flexible aerogels in solvent that are easily able to have their structure sandwiched into a 2D structure by pressure-induced rearrangement. Additionally, depending on the solvent being dissolved, they can reflect light that is iridescent and changes colour. A new generation of intelligent, mechanoresponsive photonic materials with a new response mechanism may be possible with the help of this material; an example of such efforts may be seen in ref. [427]. LC networks are interesting from mechanical property enhancement perspective as well as not only they respond to humidity with a pitch size change, but they can also maintain high elastic modulus and toughness.
